<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>This fictional modern episode addresses the presenting concerns below. [S]</paragraph><list><item>A firm lump in the left breast she found two weeks ago. [S]</item><item>Wants to be told plainly and in person what any test shows, to see the reports herself, and to make her own decisions; asks that her health be shared only with the people she chooses. [S]</item><item>Worried that illness will keep her from finishing her book. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Rachel Louise Carson was born on May 27, 1907, on a family farm in Springdale, Pennsylvania. [D]</item><item>Carson was hired by the U.S. Bureau of Fisheries in 1936 as a junior aquatic biologist and rose to be editor-in-chief of the publications of its successor, the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service. She resigned in 1952 to write full time. [D]</item><item>In 1950, while preparing her second book, Carson had her first breast operation, to remove a benign tumor from her left breast. [D]</item><item>In early 1960, while writing Silent Spring, Carson found two lumps in her left breast. [D]</item></list><paragraph>10 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

